\id ROM Transparent Bible (working title) - pilot draft
\h Romans
\toc1 The Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Romans
\toc2 Romans
\toc3 Rom
\mt1 Romans
\c 3
\s1 God’s Faithfulness and Man’s Unfaithfulness
\p
\v 1 What advantage then has the Jew? Or what is the value of circumcision?
\v 2 Much in every way. For first of all, they were entrusted with the oracles of God.
\v 3 For what if some were unfaithful? Will their unfaithfulness nullify the faithfulness of God?\f + \fr 3:3 \ft Or: \fqa did not believe \ft … \fqa unbelief \ft (KJV). Gk. \fqa apisteō\ft , \fqa apistia\ft , \fqa pistis\ft ; Paul plays on the three related words.\f*
\v 4 May it never be! Rather, let God be true, and every man a liar. As it is written,
\q1 “That you may be justified in your words,
\q2 and may prevail when you are judged.”\f + \fr 3:4 \ft Lit.: \fqa May it not be\ft ; KJV \fqa God forbid\ft . Also in vv. 6, 31.\f*\f + \fr 3:4 \ft OT: Psalm 51:4 (LXX). Or: \fqa when you judge\ft .\f*
\p
\v 5 But if our unrighteousness shows the righteousness of God, what shall we say? Is God unrighteous who inflicts wrath? (I speak in a human way.)
\v 6 May it never be! For then how will God judge the world?
\v 7 For if through my lie the truth of God has abounded to his glory, why am I still also judged as a sinner?
\v 8 And why not say—as we are slanderously reported and as some affirm that we say—“Let us do evil that good may come”? Their condemnation is just.
\s1 None Is Righteous
\p
\v 9 What then? Are we better than they? Not at all. For we have already charged both Jews and Greeks that they are all under sin.\f + \fr 3:9 \ft Or: \fqa Are we any worse off? \ft The Greek verb can be read either way.\f*
\v 10 As it is written:
\q1 “There is none righteous, not even one;
\q1
\v 11 there is none who understands;
\q2 there is none who seeks after God.
\q1
\v 12 They have all turned aside;
\q2 together they have become worthless;
\q1 there is none who does good,
\q2 there is not even one.”\f + \fr 3:12 \ft OT: vv. 10–12, Psalm 14:1–3; 53:1–3; Ecclesiastes 7:20.\f*
\q1
\v 13 “Their throat is an open grave;
\q2 with their tongues they have practiced deceit.”
\q1 “The poison of asps is under their lips.”\f + \fr 3:13 \ft OT: Psalm 5:9; 140:3.\f*
\q1
\v 14 “Their mouth is full of cursing and bitterness.”\f + \fr 3:14 \ft OT: Psalm 10:7.\f*
\q1
\v 15 “Their feet are swift to shed blood;
\q1
\v 16 destruction and misery are in their paths,
\q1
\v 17 and the way of peace they have not known.”\f + \fr 3:17 \ft OT: vv. 15–17, Isaiah 59:7–8.\f*
\q1
\v 18 “There is no fear of God before their eyes.”\f + \fr 3:18 \ft OT: Psalm 36:1.\f*
\p
\v 19 Now we know that whatever the law says, it speaks to those who are under the law, so that every mouth may be stopped, and all the world may become accountable to God.\f + \fr 3:19 \ft Lit.: \fqa in the law\f*
\v 20 For by the works of the law no flesh will be justified in his sight, for through the law comes the knowledge of sin.\f + \fr 3:20 \ft Gk.: \fqa dioti\ft , “because”; KJV \fqa Therefore\ft .\f*\f + \fr 3:20 \ft Gk.: \fqa dikaioō\ft , “justify”, that is, declare righteous. Also in vv. 24, 26, 28, 30.\f*\f + \fr 3:20 \ft Gk.: \fqa sarx\ft , “flesh”: the human being in weakness and mortality; here, “no human being”.\f*
\s1 Righteousness Through Faith
\p
\v 21 But now the righteousness of God apart from the law has been revealed, being witnessed by the Law and the Prophets,
\v 22 even the righteousness of God through the faith of Jesus Christ, to all and on all who believe. For there is no difference;\f + \fr 3:22 \ft Or: \fqa faith in Jesus Christ\ft ; or \fqa the faithfulness of Jesus Christ\ft . The Greek allows each; also in v. 26.\f*\f + \fr 3:22 \ft Text: TR, Maj.: \fqa and on all\ft ; CT omits.\f*
\v 23 for all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God,
\v 24 being justified freely by his grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus,
\v 25 whom God set forth as a propitiation through faith in his blood, to demonstrate his righteousness, because of the passing over of sins previously committed, in the forbearance of God;\f + \fr 3:25 \ft Gk.: \fqa hilastērion\ft , “propitiation”, a sacrifice that turns away wrath; the same word is used for the “mercy seat” in Hebrews 9:5. Also translated \fqa expiation\ft .\f*\f + \fr 3:25 \ft Or: \fqa a propitiation by his blood, through faith\ft .\f*\f + \fr 3:25 \ft Gk.: \fqa paresis\ft , “passing over”; KJV \fqa remission\ft .\f*
\v 26 to demonstrate, \add I say\add*, his righteousness at the present time, that he might be just and the justifier of the one who has the faith of Jesus.\f + \fr 3:26 \ft Lit.: \fqa the one of the faith of Jesus\ft ; KJV \fqa him which believeth in Jesus\ft .\f*
\p
\v 27 Where then is boasting? It is excluded. By what law? Of works? No, but by the law of faith.
\v 28 Therefore we conclude that a man is justified by faith apart from the works of the law.\f + \fr 3:28 \ft Text: TR \fqa Therefore\ft ; Maj., CT \fqa For\ft .\f*\f + \fr 3:28 \ft Gk.: \fqa logizomai\ft , “count, reckon”; see 4:3.\f*
\v 29 Or is he the God of the Jews only? Is he not also \add the God\add* of the Gentiles? Yes, of the Gentiles also,\f + \fr 3:29 \ft Gk.: \fqa ethnē\ft , “nations”.\f*
\v 30 since there is one God who will justify the circumcised by faith and the uncircumcised through faith.\f + \fr 3:30 \ft Lit.: \fqa circumcision \ft … \fqa uncircumcision\f*
\v 31 Do we then make void the law through faith? May it never be! On the contrary, we establish the law.
